export const schoolLessons=[
lesson('beowulf-story','beowulf','The story and its world','Follow the three conflicts and explain why the setting matters.',[3,8,25],[
 section('Start with the setting','Beowulf is an epic poem preserved in Old English. Its story takes place in Scandinavia. Beowulf is a Geatish warrior who travels to Denmark to help King Hrothgar. Hrothgar’s hall, Heorot (also spelled Herot in the class text), is a place for feasting, storytelling and community. Grendel’s attacks turn that shared place into a source of fear. Exposition introduces this setting, the people and the conflict.','Setting → Denmark and Geatland. Problem → Grendel threatens Hrothgar’s people. Response → Beowulf offers help.','تجري الأحداث في إسكندنافيا. يأتي بيوولف من شعب الجيت لمساعدة هروثغار في الدنمارك. القاعة رمز لاجتماع الناس وأمانهم؛ هجمات جريندل تهدد هذا الأمان.'),
 section('Follow the three battles','First, Beowulf fights Grendel in the hall without a sword and defeats him. Next, Grendel’s mother attacks in revenge, and Beowulf confronts her in her underwater home. Much later, Beowulf is king of the Geats. A stolen cup disturbs a dragon, which attacks his people. Wiglaf stays to help him when the other warriors flee. The dragon dies, but Beowulf is mortally wounded.','Sequence: Grendel → Grendel’s mother → the dragon. Wiglaf’s loyalty matters in the final battle.','ترتيب المعارك: جريندل، ثم أمه، ثم التنين بعد سنوات طويلة. يبقى ويغلاف لمساعدة بيوولف، الذي يموت متأثرًا بجراحه بعد هزيمة التنين.'),
 section('Explain the significance','A plot summary says what happens. Analysis connects an event to an idea. Beowulf’s journey can show courage and service, while his wish for fame complicates his motives. As a king, his death also leaves his people vulnerable. A strong interpretation considers both the victory and its cost instead of assuming every successful battle solves every problem.','Summary: Wiglaf stays. Analysis: His action demonstrates loyalty when loyalty carries a real risk.','الملخص يذكر الحدث، والتحليل يفسر معناه. هزيمة التنين انتصار، لكن موت الملك يترك شعبه بلا حمايته. ادعم رأيك بالفعل ونتيجته.')
],[
 mc('Who rules the Danes when Beowulf arrives to help?','Hrothgar',['Wiglaf','Grendel','The dragon'],'Hrothgar is the Danish king. Beowulf travels from Geatland to help his people.'),
 fill('Complete the sequence: Grendel, Grendel’s mother, the ___.','dragon','The dragon is Beowulf’s final opponent after he has ruled the Geats for many years.'),
 mc('Why does the hall matter beyond being a building?','It represents a community whose shared life is threatened.',['It is where Beowulf was born.','It prevents any conflict from occurring.','It belongs to Grendel’s mother.'],'Heorot hosts the community. Attacks there threaten safety, celebration and social bonds.'),
 mc('Which warrior stays to help Beowulf face the dragon?','Wiglaf',['Hrothgar','Banquo','Malcolm'],'Wiglaf remains when the other warriors flee, providing evidence of loyalty.'),
 mc('Which statement is analysis rather than just plot summary?','Wiglaf’s decision to stay shows that loyalty requires action.',['Wiglaf stays near Beowulf.','Beowulf travels to Denmark.','A dragon guards treasure.'],'Analysis explains the significance of a detail; the other options report events or facts.'),
 mc('Which conclusion includes the cost of Beowulf’s last victory?','The dragon is defeated, but the king’s death leaves his people vulnerable.',['Every danger disappears forever.','Beowulf becomes king of Denmark.','Grendel returns to guard the kingdom.'],'Victory and loss coexist: the dragon dies, but the Geats lose their protector.')
],'Explain how Beowulf’s responsibilities change from visiting warrior to king. Use two events and discuss the cost of his final victory.','A strong answer contrasts helping Hrothgar with protecting his own people, uses the dragon battle and explains the community’s vulnerability after his death.'),
lesson('epic-patterns','beowulf','Epic heroes and archetypes','Identify a pattern and support it with a specific action.',[4,5,6,7,17,25],[
 section('What makes an epic?','An epic is a long narrative poem about heroic actions and events important to a community. Epic heroes often have exceptional courage, strength or intelligence. They may travel far and face extraordinary opponents. Noble birth and high status are common conventions, not rules that every heroic story must obey. An epic hero can have weaknesses and can die.','Beowulf faces dangerous monsters to protect others; his exceptional ability serves a larger community.','الملحمة قصيدة سردية طويلة. بطلها غالبًا صاحب شجاعة أو قوة استثنائية، لكنه ليس كاملًا ولا يشترط أن يبقى حيًا في النهاية.'),
 section('Recognise recurring patterns','An archetype is a character, situation or story pattern that recurs across texts. A mentor offers guidance; a quest takes a character through trials; an opponent blocks progress. Hrothgar can be read as a wise guide. Beowulf fits a hero pattern and is also an outsider arriving from another land. These labels are ways of interpreting evidence, not complete descriptions of a person.','Claim: Hrothgar acts as a mentor. Evidence: his experience and advice guide a younger warrior.','النمط الأصلي archetype يتكرر بين القصص، مثل البطل والمرشد والرحلة. يمكن للشخصية أن تناسب أكثر من نمط؛ الدليل هو الذي يدعم التصنيف.'),
 section('Evaluate a pattern','Use claim, evidence and reasoning. Name the pattern, identify an action or scene, then explain the connection. A change of perspective can complicate your reading: one observer may call a risky fight brave, while another worries about the people left behind. Both claims need evidence. Do not treat archetypes as stereotypes about real groups of people.','A protector risks his life for others. A leader also has to consider what happens if the community loses him.','اكتب الادعاء ثم الدليل ثم التفسير. اختلاف وجهة النظر لا يعني أن أي جواب صحيح بلا دليل. الأنماط الأدبية ليست أحكامًا على جماعات حقيقية.')
],[
 mc('Which description best defines an epic?','A long narrative poem about significant heroic deeds.',['Any short poem about a personal feeling.','A list of historical dates.','A stage direction spoken by no character.'],'An epic tells a substantial heroic story, often reflecting the values of its culture.'),
 mc('A wise older figure guides a hero. Which archetype fits?','Mentor',['Antagonist','Narrator in every story','Comic villain'],'A mentor offers knowledge or guidance to support another character’s development.'),
 mc('Which claim about epic heroes is most accurate?','They can be exceptional and still have flaws.',['They must be flawless.','They never face danger.','They always survive every battle.'],'Exceptional ability does not remove weaknesses, difficult choices or the possibility of death.'),
 fill('Complete: A recurring character or story pattern is an ___.','archetype','An archetype is a recognisable pattern that appears across different stories.'),
 mc('What makes an archetype claim defensible?','A relevant scene followed by an explanation of the pattern.',['Only the character’s appearance.','The number of times a student repeats the claim.','A claim with no textual detail.'],'Evidence and reasoning connect the label to the actual character or event.'),
 mc('Why can Beowulf be both a hero and an outsider in Denmark?','He protects a community he has travelled from another land to help.',['An outsider can never be a hero.','He was born in Heorot.','Archetypes allow only one label per character.'],'Hero describes his protective role; outsider describes his position as a foreign Geat.')
],'Choose an archetype in Beowulf. Support it with a scene, then explain how another perspective might complicate the same interpretation.','Accept multiple defensible patterns. Look for a specific scene, an explained connection and an alternative perspective, not just a label.'),
lesson('character-evidence','beowulf','Characters, motives and conflicts','Distinguish a trait from a motive and support both with evidence.',[1,3,8,25],[
 section('Trait, action and motive','A trait is a characteristic such as loyalty or courage. An action is something the character does. A motive is the reason behind an action. The same action may have several motives, so use cautious language when the text does not state the reason directly. A protagonist is the central figure; an antagonist is a character or force that opposes that figure.','Wiglaf stays to fight (action). This supports loyalty (trait). His commitment to his leader helps explain his choice (motive).','الصفة مثل الوفاء، والفعل ما تقوم به الشخصية، والدافع سبب الفعل. لا تخلط بينها، ولا تفترض دافعًا بلا دليل.'),
 section('Compare the opponents','Grendel threatens the celebration and safety of Heorot; hostility to that social world shapes his role. Grendel’s mother seeks revenge for her son. The dragon reacts after a cup is stolen from its hoard. Their motives differ even though all oppose Beowulf. Calling a character an antagonist describes a narrative role, not a complete moral judgement.','Grendel’s mother → revenge. Dragon → a disturbed treasure hoard. The conflicts test the hero in different ways.','أم جريندل تتحرك للانتقام، والتنين يغضب بعد سرقة كأس من كنزه. وصف antagonist يعني المعارض للبطل، ولا يكفي وحده لتفسير كل دوافعه.'),
 section('Build a character map','Put a character at the centre. Connect a trait to an action, then add the motive and consequence. Separate what the text states from what you infer. Contrasting traits can coexist: a person can be brave and ambitious. Reconsider a claim if a later action contradicts it instead of selecting only the evidence that agrees with you.','Claim: Wiglaf is loyal. Detail: he remains during the dragon fight. Reasoning: he puts duty ahead of personal safety.','في خريطة الشخصية: صفة ← فعل يدعمها ← دافع ← نتيجة. راجع حكمك إذا ظهرت أدلة مخالفة، فقد تكون الشخصية شجاعة وطموحة معًا.')
],[
 mc('In “Wiglaf stays in the battle because he feels a duty to his king,” what is the motive?','His sense of duty',['Staying in the battle','The name Wiglaf','The place of the battle'],'The motive explains why he acts. Staying is the action itself.'),
 mc('Why does Grendel’s mother attack after Grendel’s defeat?','She seeks revenge for her son.',['She wants to open a school.','She has been named the Danish heir.','She is searching for a stolen dragon cup.'],'Her revenge creates a new conflict following the death of Grendel.'),
 fill('Complete: A character or force that opposes the protagonist is an ___.','antagonist','Antagonist describes an opposing role in a narrative; it does not by itself explain motivation.'),
 mc('Which event triggers the dragon’s attacks?','A cup is stolen from its treasure hoard.',['Hrothgar builds a school.','Wiglaf becomes a Danish king.','Grendel asks the dragon for help.'],'The theft disturbs the dragon’s hoard and leads to attacks on Beowulf’s people.'),
 mc('Which is the strongest evidence for a claim of loyalty?','A character stays to help when others run away.',['The character wears an expensive coat.','The character is tall.','The character lives near a river.'],'Remaining to help despite danger directly supports loyalty; physical details do not.'),
 mc('A later action challenges your first character judgement. What should you do?','Revise or qualify the claim using both pieces of evidence.',['Ignore the later action.','Replace all evidence with a personal opinion.','Assume characters cannot change.'],'A strong analysis considers contradictory evidence and allows a more complex conclusion.')
],'Compare the motives of two Beowulf antagonists. Explain how their different motives affect the hero’s conflicts, using relevant story details.','Look for two accurate motives, a clear comparison and discussion of their effect on conflict. Alternative readings need textual support.'),
lesson('beowulf-language','beowulf','Vocabulary and epic language','Use context to distinguish meanings and recognise poetic devices.',[3,8,21],[
 section('Six useful words','Stalk means to follow quietly without being noticed. Gruesome describes something horrifying, often involving injury. Loathsome means deeply unpleasant or disgusting. Writhing means twisting the body, often in pain. A lair is an animal’s den or a hidden resting place. Gorge has two senses: as a noun, a deep narrow valley; as a verb, to eat greedily. Use the sentence to choose the sense.','“They crossed a narrow gorge” uses the noun. “The creature would gorge on food” uses the verb.','stalk يتعقب خفية، gruesome مروّع، loathsome مقزّز، writhing يتلوى، lair عرين. gorge قد تعني واديًا ضيقًا أو يأكل بشراهة حسب السياق.'),
 section('Hear and picture the language','Alliteration repeats nearby initial consonant sounds. A simile compares using like or as. A metaphor describes one thing as another. Personification gives human qualities to something nonhuman. A kenning is a compact figurative expression, often a compound, used in place of a noun. These devices can create imagery, emphasis and a memorable rhythm.','Original examples: “bold blades” (alliteration); “the sea was a road” (metaphor); “wave-road” for sea (kenning).','الجناس الاستهلالي يكرر أصوات البداية. التشبيه يستخدم like أو as. الاستعارة تصف شيئًا بآخر. التشخيص يمنح غير الإنسان صفة بشرية. kenning تعبير مجازي مركب بدل الاسم.'),
 section('Explain an effect','Naming a device is only the first step. Point to the exact words, describe the image or sound, and connect that effect to meaning. A threatening description may create fear; a repeated sound may make a spoken passage memorable. The effect depends on the particular passage, so avoid saying that every metaphor produces the same mood.','Original example: “The wind whispered warnings.” Personification makes the surroundings seem alert to danger.','اذكر الأسلوب والكلمات الدالة عليه، ثم أثره في القارئ وصلته بالمعنى. لا تفترض أن الأسلوب نفسه يعطي دائمًا الإحساس نفسه.')
],[
 fill('Choose “lair” or “ranks”: The animal rested in its hidden ___.','lair','A lair is an animal’s den or hidden resting place. Ranks are ordered rows.'),
 mc('In “The hikers looked down into a deep gorge,” what does gorge mean?','A narrow valley with steep sides',['Eating greedily','A weapon','A crowd of warriors'],'The words looked down and deep show that gorge is a landform here.'),
 mc('Which original phrase uses alliteration?','Brave brothers battled.',['The hall was like a fortress.','The road was a ribbon.','The sea smiled at them.'],'The nearby words repeat the initial /b/ sound.'),
 mc('“The hall was like a fortress” is an example of what?','Simile',['Appositive','Past perfect','Soliloquy'],'Like introduces an explicit comparison between the hall and a fortress.'),
 fill('Choose “writhing” or “presumed”: Twisting in pain means ___.','writhing','Writhing describes a twisting bodily movement, often caused by pain.'),
 mc('Which answer goes beyond simply naming personification?','The whispering wind makes the setting seem aware of danger.',['This is personification.','The sentence has six words.','The word wind is a noun.'],'It connects the language choice to its effect and to the atmosphere of danger.')
],'Write a short heroic description using two different figurative devices and three vocabulary words. Identify the devices and explain their effects.','Check that each word fits its context, both devices are identifiable and the student explains a specific effect. Do not require imitation of a published translation.'),
lesson('half-story','half-day','Half a Day in six moments','Follow the narrator’s changing experience and recognise the ending’s surprise.',[9,11,18],[
 section('The beginning: leaving home','In Naguib Mahfouz’s “Half a Day,” a boy walks to school with his father. He enjoys his new clothes but worries about leaving home. His mother watches from a window. He sees school as frightening, while his father presents it as preparation for life. At the gate, the boy must enter on his own. The first-person narration lets us experience events through his limited understanding.','Moment 1: the walk with his father. Moment 2: entering the gate alone.','يمشي الطفل مع أبيه إلى المدرسة ويخاف الابتعاد عن البيت. السرد بضمير المتكلم يجعلنا نرى الأحداث من فهمه المحدود.'),
 section('The middle: pleasure and difficulty','The boy meets other children and begins to feel connected. School brings games, friendship and learning. It also brings rivalry, punishment, accidents and struggle. His first fear gives way to a more mixed view of experience. This combination matters: the school cannot be reduced to either complete happiness or complete misery.','Moment 3: friendships and learning. Moment 4: hardship and the need to persevere.','تجمع المدرسة الفرح والتعلم والصداقة مع المشقة والعقاب والصراع. لذلك لا يصفها النص بأنها سعادة كاملة أو تعاسة كاملة.'),
 section('The ending: recognition','When the bell rings, the narrator leaves and cannot find his father. Outside, gardens and fields have given way to buildings, traffic and crowds. A young worker offers to help him cross the road and addresses him as an old man. The surprising ending invites us to reinterpret the school day as a compressed lifetime. It is an interpretation supported by the age change, not a realistic account of normal ageing in a few hours.','Moment 5: leaving into a changed city. Moment 6: the young worker calls him “Grandpa.”','لا يجد أباه، ويرى المدينة تغيرت، ثم يخاطبه شاب بكلمة «جدي». هذه المفاجأة تجعلنا نقرأ اليوم بوصفه تمثيلًا لحياة كاملة.')
],[
 mc('Why is the boy uneasy at the beginning?','He is being separated from the familiar safety of home.',['He already knows every student.','He has finished his final school year.','He wants to fight a dragon.'],'The opening presents his anxiety about leaving home for an unfamiliar place.'),
 mc('Which pair best describes his experience inside school?','Enjoyment and hardship',['Only endless pleasure','Only complete isolation','No learning and no relationships'],'Games, learning and friendship coexist with conflict, punishment and struggle.'),
 mc('What does the narrator find when he leaves school?','A transformed, crowded city and no waiting father',['The same quiet fields with every detail unchanged','His childhood home inside the school','An empty world with no people'],'The missing father and altered surroundings prepare the reader for the final surprise.'),
 fill('Complete the form of address that signals the narrator’s old age: “___, let me take you across.”','Grandpa','The young worker’s address forces the reader to reconsider how much time has passed.'),
 mc('What does first-person narration give readers direct access to?','The narrator’s perceptions and uncertainty',['Every other character’s private thoughts','An objective explanation of every event','The exact thoughts of the absent father'],'The story is filtered through the narrator, whose understanding of events is limited.'),
 mc('Which sequence matches the story?','Walk with father → school experiences → changed city → help crossing',['Changed city → dragon battle → school → father’s return','School closes → first walk to school → new clothes → games','Help crossing → father collects him → school begins → childhood'],'The narrative moves from childhood arrival to school experiences, then the surprising changed world and ending.')
],'Explain how the final line changes your understanding of the beginning. Use one detail from the opening and one from the ending.','A strong answer connects initial dependence on the father with the later need for a young person’s help and explains the implied passage of a lifetime.'),
lesson('half-theme','half-day','From summary to thematic insight','Build and compare interpretations using patterns and evidence.',[9,12,13,18,22],[
 section('Topic is not a complete theme','A topic is a broad subject, such as time or change. A theme is a developed idea about that subject. “Time” is a topic. “People may notice major changes only after they have happened” is a thematic claim. A theme should explain details across the story rather than rely on a single word.','Topic: growth. Possible theme: gaining experience can bring both understanding and loss.','الموضوع كلمة عامة مثل الزمن. الفكرة أو الرسالة جملة تشرح رأيًا عن الموضوع. لا تكفي كلمة «الزمن» لتكون تحليلًا للرسالة.'),
 section('Trace events, patterns and changes','Start with what happens, notice repeated experiences, track changes in perspective and explain a larger idea. The narrator first fears school, then enjoys it, then discovers its difficulties. Outside, he is surprised by change he did not recognise as it happened. The ending connects personal ageing to a transformed environment.','Evidence → he cannot recognise the old street. Reasoning → his expectations no longer fit the world he encounters.','تتبع الأحداث ثم الأنماط المتكررة ثم تغير نظرة الراوي. اربط الدليل بالرسالة بتفسير، بدل وضع اقتباس دون شرح.'),
 section('Compare defensible readings','A time reading emphasises a lifetime compressed into a day. A change reading examines how experience transforms understanding. A perception reading asks why the narrator recognises change so late. These readings can coexist. Test each against the beginning, middle and ending. A personal or world connection helps only when it explains a detail in the story.','A text-to-self connection to moving schools can explain the narrator’s uncertainty; it should not replace evidence from the story.','قد تدعم القصة أكثر من تفسير: سرعة الزمن، وتغير الإنسان، وتأخر إدراك التغيير. المقارنة الجيدة تختبر كل تفسير بأدلة من القصة.')
],[
 mc('Which option is a thematic claim rather than a topic?','Experience can bring both growth and a sense of loss.',['Ageing','School','Time'],'A thematic claim states an idea about life; the other options only name subjects.'),
 mc('Which detail best supports a reading about delayed recognition of change?','The narrator is surprised that the familiar street has transformed.',['He wears new shoes.','The story is printed on paper.','The title has three words.'],'His surprise shows a gap between change occurring and his awareness of it.'),
 mc('What should follow a piece of evidence in an analytical paragraph?','Reasoning that explains how it supports the claim',['An unrelated personal story','A second claim with no connection','A claim that evidence is unnecessary'],'Reasoning connects the concrete detail to the proposed meaning.'),
 fill('Choose “topic” or “theme”: The single word “time” names a ___.','topic','A topic names a subject. A theme makes a developed claim about that subject.'),
 mc('Two interpretations explain different patterns in the story. What is the best next step?','Compare how well each fits evidence across the story.',['Reject both because only one interpretation is possible.','Choose the longer answer automatically.','Ignore all evidence and vote.'],'Interpretations can coexist, but each must be tested against relevant details.'),
 mc('Which use of a personal connection strengthens analysis?','Explaining how changing schools helps you understand the narrator’s fear.',['Writing only about your own holiday','Replacing the ending with your memories','Claiming your experience proves the author’s private intentions'],'A useful connection illuminates the text rather than replacing textual analysis.')
],'Judge this claim: “Growing older in Half a Day means both gaining experience and losing familiarity.” Agree, partly agree or disagree with evidence from three stages of the story.','Grade the reasoning and evidence rather than the chosen position. Look for a qualification, a pattern and consideration of the ending.'),
lesson('half-symbols','half-day','Symbols and the changed world','Explain a symbolic interpretation and its limits.',[11,12,13,18,22],[
 section('Move from object to meaning','A symbol is a concrete object, place, action or image that suggests a broader meaning. Identify the literal detail first, then explain the idea it may represent. Context supports the connection. A gate may function as a boundary in one story without meaning the same thing in every text.','The school gate literally separates inside from outside. Symbolically, it can mark a transition into a new stage of experience.','الرمز شيء ملموس يحمل معنى أوسع. ابدأ بمعناه الحقيقي، ثم فسر المعنى المحتمل بدليل من السياق.'),
 section('Read the symbols together','The school can represent a small version of life, with relationships, pleasure, learning and difficulty. It can also mark a boundary between childhood and later experience. The journey home suggests transition, while the changed city suggests time and social transformation. The final crossing reverses the opening dependence: a child once needed his father; an older man now needs a young person.','School → mixed life experiences. Changed city → transformation. Crossing → continuing dependence across stages of life.','يمكن أن تمثل المدرسة الحياة بمسراتها وصعوباتها. تغير المدينة يدل على التحول، ومساعدة الشاب تقابل اعتماد الطفل على أبيه في البداية.'),
 section('Avoid over-simple interpretations','A classroom slide may describe school as protection or innocence. The story also includes punishment and conflict, so qualify that interpretation. Symbolism gains depth when you explain what a reading captures and what complicates it. Do not assign a single fixed meaning without considering the whole story.','“School suggests childhood, but its hardships also make it a model of adult life” includes supporting and complicating details.','لا تختزل المدرسة في الحماية فقط؛ ففيها عقاب وصراع أيضًا. التحليل الأقوى يوضح ما يدعم التفسير وما يجعله أكثر تعقيدًا.')
],[
 mc('What is the first step in explaining a symbol?','Identify the literal detail before proposing its broader meaning.',['Guess the author’s private life','Choose a meaning unrelated to context','Count only the letters in the word'],'A symbolic interpretation starts from a concrete detail and is supported by its context.'),
 mc('Which detail complicates the claim that school represents only safety?','The narrator experiences punishment and conflict there.',['The school has a gate.','The father walks.','The narrator wears clothes.'],'Punishment and conflict challenge an interpretation of the school as entirely safe or innocent.'),
 mc('What does the contrast between gardens and the crowded city support?','A reading about time and social transformation',['A claim that no change occurs','A claim that the whole story is a travel guide','A claim that traffic existed only in the narrator’s imagination'],'The visible environmental change reinforces the story’s concerns with transformation and time.'),
 fill('Complete: A concrete detail that suggests a broader meaning is a ___.','symbol','A symbol retains its literal role while also suggesting an idea supported by context.'),
 mc('How does the final crossing connect to the opening?','Both show a person needing another person’s guidance.',['Both show the narrator defeating a monster.','Both happen inside the same classroom.','Both prove he is still the same age.'],'The father’s guidance at the beginning is echoed and reversed by the young person’s assistance at the end.'),
 mc('Which statement is the most careful symbolic reading?','The school can represent life because it includes joy and struggle.',['Every school in every story always means death.','The gate must mean exactly one thing.','Symbols never need evidence.'],'It proposes a meaning and supports it with the school’s mixed experiences.')
],'Explain how the school, the changed city and the final crossing work together. Include a detail that complicates a simple “childhood is safe” interpretation.','Look for linked symbols, accurate details and recognition that the school includes hardship as well as comfort. Accept different well-supported readings.'),
lesson('half-vocabulary','half-day','Crowds, connotation and context','Choose words that communicate both meaning and attitude.',[9,11,18,20,24],[
 section('Three words for groups','Ranks suggests ordered lines or rows. Throngs means large crowds, often moving together, and is relatively neutral unless the context adds an attitude. Hordes suggests a very large, potentially overwhelming or disorderly group and often has a negative connotation. Connotation is an association or feeling attached to a word, beyond its basic definition.','“Ranks of pupils” stresses organisation. “Throngs at the gate” stresses numbers. “Hordes invading the street” sounds overwhelming.','ranks صفوف منظمة، throngs حشود، hordes حشود ضخمة قد تبدو فوضوية أو طاغية. الإيحاء connotation هو الشعور الذي تضيفه الكلمة.'),
 section('Feelings, assumptions and effort','Misgivings are doubts or worries. Presumed means assumed something was true without complete proof. Perseverance is continued effort despite difficulty. In the story, early misgivings change as relationships develop, but later hardship demands perseverance. A change in attitude does not mean every early worry was foolish.','Original example: “She had misgivings about the task, but perseverance helped her finish it.”','misgivings شكوك ومخاوف، presumed افترض، perseverance مثابرة رغم الصعوبة. استخدم معنى الكلمة داخل جملة كاملة.'),
 section('Useful words for discussing syntax','Components are the parts of a whole. Complementary things work well together. Joined means connected; repeatedly means again and again; overall means considering the whole. Do not confuse complementary with complimentary, which can mean expressing praise or provided free. Choose a word by both its meaning and its grammatical role.','Original example: “The components were joined carefully, and the overall result was strong.”','components مكونات، complementary متكامل، joined متصل، repeatedly مرارًا، overall إجماليًا. complementary تختلف عن complimentary.')
],[
 fill('Choose “ranks” or “hordes”: The pupils stood in neat ___ for assembly.','ranks','Neat organisation points to ranks, which suggests ordered rows.'),
 mc('Which word most strongly suggests an overwhelming, disorderly crowd?','Hordes',['Ranks','Components','Perseverance'],'Hordes often adds a more negative or overwhelming association than the more neutral throngs.'),
 mc('What are misgivings?','Doubts or worries',['Confident proofs','Parts of an engine','Rows of soldiers'],'Misgivings are uneasy feelings of doubt about something.'),
 fill('Complete with the word meaning continued effort despite difficulty: ___.','perseverance','Perseverance means continuing to make an effort even when something is difficult.'),
 mc('Two design styles work well together. They are ___.','complementary',['complimentary','presumed','gruesome'],'Complementary means fitting or working well together; complimentary has different meanings.'),
 mc('Why might replacing “throngs” with “hordes” change a description?','It can make the same crowd sound more threatening or overwhelming.',['It always makes the number exact.','It changes every noun into a verb.','It removes the narrator’s attitude.'],'Near-synonyms can have different connotations and therefore shape the reader’s response differently.')
],'Describe the same crowd twice, first using “ranks” and then “hordes.” Explain how the change in word choice changes the reader’s impression.','Assess whether the two descriptions fit each word’s connotation and whether the comparison explains attitude, not only crowd size.'),
lesson('syntax','language','How syntax shapes a voice','Explain how sentence structure affects emphasis and pace.',[2,4,17,20],[
 section('Structure creates an effect','Syntax is the arrangement of words, phrases and clauses. Diction is word choice. Style combines these choices with features such as tone and imagery. To analyse syntax, identify a particular structure, show an example and explain its effect in context.','Original example: “The gate opened. Nobody moved.” Two short sentences separate action from the reaction.','syntax تركيب الجملة، diction اختيار الكلمات. اربط التركيب بأثره في هذا السياق، وليس بقاعدة ثابتة.'),
 section('Length is a choice','A short sentence can emphasise a sudden event. A longer sentence can accumulate details or follow a train of thought. Length alone does not determine speed: punctuation, verbs and context also matter. A fragment is incomplete as a sentence; in literature it may be deliberate, but use complete sentences in formal responses unless there is a clear reason.','“She waited, listening to the rain while the hallway emptied.” The added clauses extend our attention to the wait.','الجملة القصيرة قد تؤكد لحظة، والطويلة قد تجمع التفاصيل. ليس كل طويل بطيئًا ولا كل قصير سريعًا.'),
 section('Connections and emphasis','Transitions show relationships: however signals contrast, therefore a result, and meanwhile simultaneous events. Repetition can emphasise an idea. Parallel structure gives related items similar grammatical forms. Explain why a choice matters instead of merely naming it.','“We read carefully, discuss openly and revise patiently.” Three parallel verb phrases stress a shared process.','however للتباين، therefore للنتيجة، meanwhile للتزامن. لا يكفي تسمية الأسلوب؛ فسّر أثره.')
],[
 mc('Which term means the arrangement of words and clauses?','Syntax',['Diction','Setting','Theme'],'Syntax concerns structure; diction concerns vocabulary choices.'),
 mc('Which transition explicitly signals a contrast?','However',['Therefore','Meanwhile','Consequently'],'However introduces a contrasting idea.'),
 mc('Which comment analyses rather than only labels syntax?','The isolated short sentence makes the sudden silence stand out.',['There is a sentence.','The author uses words.','This text has punctuation.'],'It identifies a structural choice and explains its contextual effect.'),
 mc('Which statement about sentence length is best supported?','Its effect depends on punctuation, content and context.',['Long sentences are always bad.','Short sentences always create speed.','Length never affects emphasis.'],'Avoid fixed rules: several features work together to shape pace and emphasis.'),
 fill('Choose “syntax” or “diction”: Replacing “walked” with “staggered” changes ___.','diction','Changing the selected word is a choice of diction.'),
 mc('Which sentence uses parallel verb phrases?','They plan carefully, practise daily and reflect honestly.',['They plan carefully, daily practice and honest.','They planning, practise and reflection.','Planning carefully, they daily and honesty.'],'Plan, practise and reflect follow the same grammatical pattern.')
],'Write two versions of a moment of suspense: one with short sentences and one with a longer sentence. Explain how the syntax changes the effect.','Look for intentional structural differences and an explanation tied to the actual wording, not a rigid claim that long always means slow.'),
lesson('appositives','language','Appositives: name it again','Identify noun phrases that rename nouns and punctuate them in context.',[23],[
 section('A noun phrase beside a noun','An appositive is a noun or noun phrase that renames another noun. It is not simply any extra information. A relative clause uses a relative word and a verb; it can describe a noun but is not an appositive noun phrase.','“Maya, our captain, spoke.” → our captain renames Maya. “Maya, who leads our team, spoke.” → who leads our team is a relative clause.','البدل هنا اسم أو عبارة اسمية تعيد تسمية اسم. who leads our team جملة وصفية فيها فعل، وليست عبارة بدل اسمية.'),
 section('Extra information needs commas','Use commas around a nonessential appositive: it adds information but is not needed to identify the noun in this context. If it appears in the middle, use a pair of commas. If it ends the sentence, the closing punctuation replaces the second comma.','“Our only coach, Ms Ali, arrived.” Only coach already identifies the person, so the name is extra information.','المعلومة الإضافية غير الضرورية لتحديد المقصود تُحاط بفاصلتين إذا كانت وسط الجملة.'),
 section('Essential information changes punctuation','An essential appositive identifies which person or thing is meant, so it is not set off with commas. The same words can need different punctuation in different situations. Ask whether the reader already knows exactly which noun is meant.','There are several writers in the group: “The writer Lina presented first.” Lina identifies which writer.','إذا كان الاسم ضروريًا لتحديد المقصود فلا نفصله بفواصل. السياق هو الذي يحدد الضرورة.')
],[
 mc('What renames Maya in “Maya, our captain, spoke”?','our captain',['spoke','Maya spoke','the comma'],'Our captain is a noun phrase that renames Maya.'),
 mc('Which is a relative clause rather than an appositive noun phrase?','who leads our team',['our captain','a talented speaker','the school librarian'],'Who leads our team contains a relative pronoun and a verb.'),
 mc('A school has exactly one principal. Choose the punctuation for extra information.','Our only principal, Mr Noor, welcomed us.',['Our only principal Mr Noor, welcomed us.','Our only principal, Mr Noor welcomed us.','Our, only principal Mr Noor welcomed us.'],'The identifying phrase our only principal makes the name nonessential here; use two commas.'),
 mc('Several pupils are waiting. The name identifies which pupil. Choose the sentence.','The pupil Sara entered first.',['The pupil, Sara, entered first.','The pupil Sara, entered first.','The pupil, Sara entered first.'],'Sara is essential to identify which pupil in this stated context.'),
 fill('An appositive renames a noun using a noun or noun ___.','phrase','An appositive can be one noun or a longer noun phrase.'),
 mc('Why should punctuation be checked in context?','The information may be essential in one context and extra in another.',['Every name always needs commas.','Commas are optional decoration.','Every phrase containing who is an appositive.'],'Whether the reader can identify the noun without the appositive determines the distinction.')
],'Write one sentence with an essential appositive and one with a nonessential appositive. State the context that justifies each punctuation choice.','Check noun-phrase form, identification in the stated context and paired commas for a middle nonessential appositive.'),
lesson('parallel-actions','language','Diazeugma & parallel actions','Use one subject with several verbs clearly.',[25,4],[
 section('One subject, several actions','Diazeugma is a construction in which one subject governs several verbs. It can emphasise a sequence of actions or a character’s activity. Identify the shared subject and each action before discussing an effect.','“The warrior entered, listened and waited.” The warrior is the shared subject of all three verbs.','diazeugma فاعل واحد يرتبط بأفعال متعددة. حدّد الفاعل ثم الأفعال قبل تفسير الأثر.'),
 section('Parallelism makes actions clear','Related actions often use parallel grammatical forms. When the sentence describes completed actions in the past, keep the verbs consistently in the past unless the meaning requires a change. Parallelism is broader than diazeugma: it can also join nouns, adjectives or clauses.','“She checked the map, packed her bag and left.” Each past-tense verb shares the subject she.','التوازي أوسع من تعدد أفعال الفاعل الواحد؛ يمكن أن يظهر في أسماء أو صفات أو جمل.'),
 section('Analyse the sequence','A series of active verbs can build momentum, but its actual effect depends on meaning. A sequence such as paused, considered and waited may slow the action. Describe the pattern and explain why those particular actions matter.','“He searched, climbed and reached the summit” moves toward a goal; “he paused, sighed and waited” delays it.','معنى الأفعال يحدد الأثر. لا تفترض أن كل سلسلة أفعال تعني حركة سريعة.')
],[
 mc('In “The warrior entered, listened and waited,” what is the shared subject?','The warrior',['entered','listened','waited'],'All three verbs have the warrior as their subject.'),
 fill('How many verbs share that subject in “She checked, packed and left”? Write a number.',['3','three'],'Checked, packed and left are three verbs.'),
 mc('Which sentence clearly uses one subject with several parallel past-tense verbs?','Omar opened the book, found the page and read the passage.',['Omar opened the book, finding the page and read.','Omar open the book, found and reading.','Omar, the book and the page.'],'Opened, found and read all refer to completed actions by Omar.'),
 mc('Which distinction is accurate?','Parallelism can involve nouns or clauses as well as verbs.',['Parallelism only means one subject with verbs.','Diazeugma has no verbs.','Parallel structures must be questions.'],'Diazeugma is a specific shared-subject construction; parallelism is a broader structural pattern.'),
 mc('Which series most clearly delays action?','He paused, considered and waited.',['He sprinted, leapt and landed.','He ran, climbed and jumped.','He charged, struck and pursued.'],'Pausing, considering and waiting explicitly postpone further action.'),
 mc('What should an analysis of a verb sequence include?','The shared subject, the actions and their effect in context.',['Only a count of commas.','An unrelated plot summary.','A claim that every sequence creates speed.'],'Link the actual construction to the meaning it creates.')
],'Describe a character making a difficult decision using one subject and at least three verbs. Identify the verbs and explain the effect of their order.','Require a clear shared subject and a meaningful explanation of sequence. Accept intentional tense changes if justified by time.'),
lesson('narrative-tense','language','Time, tense & perspective','Keep time relationships clear when narrating and analysing.',[12,13,22],[
 section('Past and present','The simple past can narrate completed events. The present can narrate for immediacy or state an interpretation in literary analysis. A literary present describes what a work does: “The ending changes our view.” Do not change tense accidentally within the same time frame.','Past narration: “He crossed the yard.” Literary analysis: “The yard represents a threshold.”','الماضي يسرد حدثًا انتهى، والحاضر يستخدم في التحليل الأدبي أو السرد المباشر. تجنب تغيير الزمن بلا سبب.'),
 section('Earlier than a past moment','The past perfect, had + past participle, marks an event completed before another past moment when that relationship needs emphasis. Once the sequence is clear, writers do not need to put every earlier sentence in the past perfect.','“When she returned, the crowd had left.” Leaving happened before her return.','الماضي التام had + التصريف الثالث يوضح حدثًا سبق نقطة ماضية أخرى.'),
 section('Intentional shifts','A purposeful shift may mark a memory, a change of frame or a general truth. Ask what each tense refers to. In Half a Day, narrative compression and the final encounter reshape our perception of time; the surprise is not explained merely by spotting a verb tense.','“I remembered the school I had entered that morning.” The entering precedes the remembering.','التغيير المتعمد قد يشير لذكرى أو إطار جديد. تأثير الزمن في القصة أوسع من تصريف الأفعال وحده.')
],[
 fill('Complete with the past perfect of “leave”: By the time I arrived, they ___ ___.','had left','Had left puts the leaving before the past arrival.'),
 mc('Which sentence uses the literary present?','The ending challenges the reader’s expectations.',['The reader finished yesterday.','The class had left before noon.','The book was printed last year.'],'Challenges describes what the ending does in an analysis.'),
 mc('In “When she returned, the crowd had left,” which event happened first?','The crowd left.',['She returned.','Both necessarily happened at the same instant.','Neither happened.'],'Past perfect marks the leaving as earlier than the past return.'),
 mc('Which revision keeps a completed past sequence consistent?','He opened the gate and walked inside.',['He opened the gate and walks inside yesterday.','He opens yesterday and walking.','He opening the gate and walked.'],'Opened and walked are consistent past-tense actions in the same frame.'),
 mc('Which tense shift has an explicit reason?','She remembered that she had lost the key earlier.',['She walked yesterday and suddenly walks, with no frame change.','She opened and opening.','She had and will was.'],'Had lost marks an event before the past remembering.'),
 mc('Why is a tense checklist alone insufficient for analysing Half a Day?','Symbolism, compressed events and the ending also shape its treatment of time.',['Verb tense determines every theme.','The ending has no relevance.','Stories never use time relationships.'],'Time is built through narrative structure and perspective as well as grammar.')
],'Write a short memory in the past, using the past perfect to show one earlier event. Then explain why the shift is needed.','Check past perfect form and a genuinely earlier event. Reward a clear explanation of the time relationship.'),
lesson('macbeth-act-one','drama','Macbeth: the choices in Act 1','Follow the movement from public honour to private intention.',[8,15],[
 section('Honour and prediction','In Act 1, a captain describes Macbeth’s bravery in battle. The witches greet him with titles, including Thane of Cawdor and future king. News confirms Cawdor, making the prediction of kingship more tempting. A prediction influences him; it does not remove his responsibility for choosing how to act.','Sequence: reputation for bravery → predictions → confirmed title → growing thoughts of kingship.','يبدأ ماكبث محاربًا شجاعًا. تحقق لقب كودور يجعل النبوءة مغرية، لكنه يظل مسؤولًا عن قراراته.'),
 section('Appearances and intentions','Duncan names Malcolm his heir, an obstacle to Macbeth’s ambition. Lady Macbeth reads her husband’s letter and urges action. Duncan arrives as a welcomed guest while the audience knows the hosts’ dangerous intentions. This gap in knowledge creates dramatic irony.','Dramatic irony: a character trusts a situation that the audience has reason to fear.','المفارقة الدرامية: يعرف الجمهور ما لا تعرفه الشخصية. استقبال دنكن يبدو آمنًا بينما نعرف الخطر.'),
 section('The decision in Scene 7','Macbeth considers his duties as Duncan’s subject, relative and host, and recognises Duncan’s virtues. Ambition pulls against these obligations. Lady Macbeth attacks his courage and outlines a plan; he resolves to proceed. Act 1 ends with that decision. Duncan’s murder happens later, not in Act 1.','A useful analysis distinguishes pressure from choice: Lady Macbeth influences him, but Macbeth agrees.','في المشهد السابع يتردد بسبب الواجب والضيافة وفضائل دنكن، ثم يقرر التنفيذ. القتل ليس في الفصل الأول.')
],[
 mc('What first makes the witches’ prediction seem more credible to Macbeth?','He receives the title Thane of Cawdor.',['Duncan is killed in Scene 1.','Malcolm gives him the crown.','The witches crown him publicly.'],'Confirmation of Cawdor makes the remaining promise of kingship more tempting.'),
 mc('Who is named Duncan’s heir in Act 1?','Malcolm',['Macbeth','Banquo','Macduff'],'Naming Malcolm as heir places an obstacle between Macbeth and the crown.'),
 mc('Why is Duncan’s arrival an example of dramatic irony?','The audience knows of dangerous intentions that Duncan does not recognise.',['Everyone has identical knowledge.','Duncan announces the murder plan.','The scene contains no contrast.'],'The gap between Duncan’s trust and the audience’s knowledge creates irony.'),
 mc('Which obligation does Macbeth recognise in Scene 7?','As Duncan’s host, he should protect him.',['As host, he must attack every guest.','As a warrior, he has no moral duties.','As a subject, he must obey the witches.'],'Hospitality is one of his reasons against the murder.'),
 mc('What has happened by the end of Act 1?','Macbeth has agreed to proceed with the murder plan.',['Duncan has already been murdered.','Macbeth has defeated Macduff.','Banquo has become king.'],'The act ends with intention and planning, before the later murder.'),
 mc('Which statement best preserves Macbeth’s agency?','The prophecy and Lady Macbeth influence him, but he chooses to act.',['The witches physically force every action.','He has no alternatives or doubts.','His ambition never matters.'],'The act dramatises temptation, moral reasoning and a choice.')
],'Explain Macbeth’s conflict in Act 1, Scene 7. Use two specific reasons for hesitation and explain why his final decision remains his responsibility.','Accept duty, kinship, hospitality, Duncan’s virtue or consequences, supported by accurate references. Distinguish influence from compulsion.'),
lesson('tragic-heroes','drama','Epic hero or tragic hero?','Compare heroic achievement with a downfall shaped by choices.',[5,6,7,15],[
 section('Patterns, not rigid boxes','An epic hero often performs exceptional deeds in a story linked to a community’s values. A tragic hero’s downfall is significantly shaped by a flaw or serious error, alongside circumstances. Both kinds of character can face inner and outer conflict. A heroic death alone does not make a character a tragic hero.','Beowulf’s final battle protects his people, though his death leaves them vulnerable. Macbeth’s destructive choices help create his downfall.','موت البطل لا يجعله بطلًا تراجيديًا تلقائيًا. انظر إلى أسباب السقوط وصلته باختياراته.'),
 section('Vocabulary of tragedy','Hamartia refers to a tragic error or flaw; hubris to excessive pride. Peripeteia is a reversal of fortune, and anagnorisis is a recognition or discovery. Catharsis describes the audience’s emotional response, traditionally discussed through pity and fear. These terms name different parts of the experience.','Error → consequences → reversal can be a useful pattern, but verify it against the particular work.','hamartia خطأ أو عيب مأساوي، hubris غرور مفرط، peripeteia انقلاب الحظ، anagnorisis انكشاف، catharsis تطهير أو استجابة وجدانية.'),
 section('Compare with evidence','Start with one criterion, such as responsibility or relationship with the community. Give evidence for each character, then explain the similarity or difference. Do not reduce the comparison to “one is good, one is bad.” Early bravery and later choices can coexist in the same person.','Claim: both characters have public status. Difference: the consequences of their choices affect that status and their communities in different ways.','قارن وفق معيار محدد مع دليل لكل شخصية. لا تختصر الشخصيات في جيد وسيئ.')
],[
 mc('Does dying in battle automatically make a character a tragic hero?','No; the causes and pattern of the downfall must be examined.',['Yes, every death is a tragic flaw.','Yes, death proves excessive pride.','No hero can ever die.'],'A tragic pattern concerns causation and character, not death alone.'),
 fill('Which term means excessive pride: hubris or catharsis?','hubris','Hubris means excessive pride; catharsis concerns the audience’s emotional response.'),
 mc('Which term names a reversal of fortune?','Peripeteia',['Anagnorisis','Alliteration','Kenning'],'Peripeteia is reversal; anagnorisis is recognition.'),
 mc('What does hamartia refer to?','A serious error or flaw contributing to a tragic downfall.',['Any supernatural weapon','A repeated initial sound','The physical location of a scene'],'Hamartia connects a character’s error or flaw to the tragic outcome.'),
 mc('Which comparison offers the strongest analytical method?','Choose one criterion and support both sides with specific evidence.',['List unrelated adjectives without evidence.','Assume all epic heroes are morally perfect.','Treat every conflict as identical.'],'A shared criterion gives the comparison focus, and evidence supports its claims.'),
 mc('Which statement avoids a false distinction?','Epic and tragic heroes can both experience internal and external conflict.',['Epic heroes never think or doubt.','Tragic heroes never face opponents.','Only tragic heroes have communities.'],'The patterns differ, but inner and outer conflicts are not exclusive to either.')
],'Compare Beowulf and Macbeth using responsibility to the community as your criterion. Include one specific event for each, and qualify any oversimplified conclusion.','Assess accurate evidence, a shared criterion and thoughtful comparison. Accept defensible nuance about glory, vulnerability and mixed motives.'),
lesson('soliloquy','drama','Hearing a character think','Explain what a soliloquy reveals that public behaviour can hide.',[15,19],[
 section('Private thought made audible','A soliloquy is dramatic speech that expresses a character’s thoughts aloud, conventionally unheard by other characters. The character is often alone, but physical solitude is not the only defining feature. The audience gains access to private conflict. A long public speech is not automatically a soliloquy.','A character weighing a secret choice aloud while others do not hear is different from addressing an assembled crowd.','المناجاة تفكير الشخصية بصوت يسمعه الجمهور ولا تسمعه الشخصيات الأخرى عادةً. ليست كل خطبة طويلة مناجاة.'),
 section('Speech and silent thought','An internal monologue can present thought silently in narrative. A soliloquy is spoken in drama. An aside is usually a shorter remark intended for the audience or selected listeners. Ask who can hear the words and what information the audience gains.','Silent narration: “She wondered whether to leave.” Dramatic thought aloud can reveal the same uncertainty through voice.','الحوار الداخلي قد يكون صامتًا في السرد؛ المناجاة خطاب مسموع على المسرح. اسأل: من يسمع؟ وماذا نعرف؟'),
 section('Macbeth’s divided reasoning','In Act 1, Scene 7, Macbeth’s private speech weighs consequences, loyalty and hospitality against ambition. That access makes his later decision more significant: the audience knows he has identified reasons not to act. Analyse a specific conflict and explain its effect on the audience.','A response can trace duty → objection → ambition, then discuss how hearing the reasoning shapes our judgment.','نعرف من تردده أنه يفهم الأسباب الأخلاقية لعدم القتل. هذا يجعل قراره اللاحق أكثر دلالة.')
],[
 mc('What is central to a soliloquy?','A character expresses private thoughts aloud for the audience.',['A narrator lists stage equipment.','Any speech longer than one minute.','All characters discuss a public vote.'],'The convention makes private thought audible to the audience.'),
 mc('Which question helps identify the dramatic convention?','Who can hear the speech within the scene?',['How many pages does the book have?','What is the cover colour?','How tall is the theatre?'],'Audience and character access to the speech are crucial.'),
 mc('Which is an internal monologue presented silently rather than a spoken soliloquy?','The narrator reports the character’s unspoken thoughts.',['The actor voices private doubt aloud.','A character speaks thoughts alone onstage.','The audience hears a character weigh a secret choice.'],'Unspoken thought in narration differs from voiced dramatic thought.'),
 mc('Why does Macbeth’s hesitation matter to the audience?','It shows that he recognises moral objections before choosing to proceed.',['It proves that he has no choice.','It means Duncan has already been killed.','It removes all conflict from the scene.'],'Hearing his reasoning reveals awareness and responsibility.'),
 mc('Which speech is least likely to be a soliloquy?','A captain formally addresses the soldiers, who listen and reply.',['A lone character voices a secret fear.','A character weighs a private choice unheard by others.','A character speaks inner doubts for the audience.'],'The captain is openly communicating with other characters, not voicing private thought under the convention.'),
 mc('Which approach best analyses a soliloquy?','Identify the private conflict and explain what the audience learns.',['Summarise the entire play without discussing the speech.','Count words and stop.','Assume private speech is always truthful in every detail.'],'Analysis connects the speech’s content and dramatic function.')
],'Write a short original soliloquy for a student tempted to hide a mistake. Add a note explaining the inner conflict and what the audience learns.','Evaluate a voiced private conflict, a meaningful choice and an explanation of dramatic function. No single decision or interpretation is mandatory.'),
lesson('ielts-intro','ielts','IELTS: know the task','Understand the four skills and distinguish evidence from assumptions.',[14],[
 section('Four skills, two common versions','IELTS assesses Listening, Reading, Writing and Speaking. Academic and General Training use different Reading and Writing tasks, while Listening and Speaking are the same. Choose the version required by the institution or purpose. Requirements vary; there is no universal passing band.','This lesson is familiarisation, not an official IELTS test or a band prediction. Check current requirements with the organisation receiving your results.','الآيلتس يقيس أربع مهارات. متطلبات الدرجة والنوع تختلف حسب الجهة. هذا تدريب تعريفي وليس اختبارًا رسميًا.'),
 section('Know the format','Listening has four parts and 40 questions; the recording lasts about 30 minutes and is heard once. Reading has 40 questions in 60 minutes. Writing takes 60 minutes for two tasks. In Academic Writing, Task 1 describes visual information and Task 2 develops an argument; Task 2 carries more weight. Speaking has three parts and lasts 11–14 minutes. Delivery and transfer arrangements depend on the test mode.','Plan time for both writing tasks. Practice here has no listening recording and does not simulate the full test.','الكتابة 60 دقيقة لمهمتين، والثانية وزنها أكبر. لا يوجد تسجيل استماع أو محاكاة كاملة في هذا الدرس.'),
 section('Read for evidence','Skim to identify the main idea; scan to locate a detail. Look for paraphrases, not just identical words. For True / False / Not Given: True agrees with the passage, False contradicts it, and Not Given lacks enough evidence. Yes / No / Not Given similarly concerns a writer’s views or claims. Do not use outside knowledge to fill missing information.','Original practice text: “The reading club meets on Tuesdays. It began in September. Members can borrow two books at a time.” The text says nothing about the club’s membership fee.','True يوافق النص، False يناقضه، Not Given لا توجد معلومات كافية. لا تضف معلومة من خارج النص.'),
 section('What quality means','Writing is assessed through task achievement or response, coherence and cohesion, vocabulary, and grammatical range and accuracy. Speaking also considers fluency and pronunciation. Clear development and relevant examples matter more than memorised impressive words. This app’s teacher rubric is a classroom practice rubric, not an IELTS band scale.','For a short practice argument: give a position, a reason, a concrete example and a brief qualification.','وضوح الفكرة وتنظيمها ودليلها مهم. درجة الأستاذ في التطبيق ليست درجة IELTS.')
],[
 mc('Which two sections differ between Academic and General Training?','Reading and Writing',['Listening and Speaking','Listening and Reading','Speaking and Writing'],'The Reading and Writing tasks differ; Listening and Speaking are shared.'),
 mc('In Academic Writing, which task carries more weight?','Task 2',['Task 1','Both always have equal weight','Neither is assessed'],'Task 2 carries more weight, so it needs sufficient time and development.'),
 mc('Passage: “The reading club meets on Tuesdays.” Statement: “The club meets on Tuesdays.”','True',['False','Not Given','Impossible to classify'],'The statement directly agrees with the given passage.'),
 mc('Passage: “Members can borrow two books at a time.” Statement: “Members can borrow only one book at a time.”','False',['True','Not Given','Both True and False'],'Only one contradicts the stated allowance of two.'),
 mc('Passage: “The club began in September and meets on Tuesdays.” Statement: “Membership is free.”','Not Given',['True','False','Always True for school clubs'],'Neither the starting month nor the meeting day tells us the price.'),
 mc('What is scanning used for?','Finding a particular detail quickly.',['Memorising every sentence before reading.','Replacing evidence with a guess.','Determining an official band from one answer.'],'Scanning focuses on locating a specific detail; skimming gives a general overview.')
],'Some people believe every secondary-school student should join an activity club. Write a short argument giving your position, a reason, an example and a qualification. Aim for 120–180 words for this classroom practice; it is not a full IELTS task.','Use the classroom rubric only. Judge relevance, development, organisation and language; do not assign or imply an official IELTS band.'),
lesson('project-cycle','projects','From club idea to presentation','Plan a useful product, develop it and revise from evidence.',[10],[
 section('Define the audience and purpose','Begin with the audience, the need and the purpose. The activity-club project can become a magazine, a journalism piece, a short talk or a Shakespeare adaptation. Choose a format because it serves the idea. An adaptation may update the setting while retaining a central conflict.','A school magazine article about study spaces needs a clear audience and a question it will answer.','ابدأ بالجمهور والهدف والحاجة، ثم اختر شكل المشروع المناسب. الاقتباس المسرحي يمكنه تغيير المكان مع الحفاظ على الصراع.'),
 section('Research and build','Assign clear roles and deadlines, but keep shared responsibility. Check sources before using them. KEEP information that is credible and relevant; CHECK uncertain claims against reliable sources; REJECT unsupported or irrelevant claims. Distinguish your evidence from your own opinion and credit the source.','A striking claim with no identifiable source belongs in CHECK, not automatically in the final slide.','وزّع الأدوار وحدد المواعيد. احتفظ بالمعلومة الموثوقة، تحقق من المشكوك فيها، واستبعد غير المسندة أو غير المناسبة.'),
 section('Revise and rehearse','The four-session cycle moves from planning to a draft, then feedback and improvement, then presentation and reflection. Ask peers for two specific strengths and one actionable improvement. Rehearse transitions and timing. The source project rubric uses four categories of five points; this app’s written-response rubric is a separate practice scale.','“Your examples are relevant; add a source under the statistic on slide 3” gives a specific next action.','خطط ثم أنشئ مسودة، خذ ملاحظات وحسّن، ثم قدم وتأمل. مقياس المهمة الكتابية هنا منفصل عن مقياس المشروع الأصلي.')
],[
 mc('What should guide the choice of project format?','The audience and purpose',['Only the longest possible file','The number of animations available','Avoiding all research'],'A format is useful when it helps communicate the intended message to its audience.'),
 mc('An interesting statistic has no identifiable source. What is the next step?','CHECK it against a reliable source.',['Publish it as certain.','Invent a source name.','Repeat it more loudly.'],'Unverified information requires checking before it becomes evidence.'),
 mc('Which sequence matches the project cycle?','Plan → draft → feedback and revision → present and reflect',['Present → ignore feedback → invent evidence → plan','Grade → publish → choose an audience → draft','Decorate → stop → avoid rehearsal → submit'],'The cycle develops the work and uses feedback before final presentation.'),
 mc('Which peer comment is actionable?','Add a source under the statistic on slide 3.',['It is bad.','Whatever.','Make everything perfect.'],'It names a specific issue and a change the team can make.'),
 mc('What can a thoughtful Shakespeare adaptation preserve while changing the setting?','The central conflict',['Every original costume necessarily','Only the number of pages','Nothing about the characters’ choices'],'A new setting can still explore the original conflict and choices.'),
 mc('Why rehearse the whole presentation together?','To check timing, transitions and how the parts fit.',['To remove every team member’s role.','To avoid checking facts.','To guarantee a particular grade.'],'A shared rehearsal reveals gaps that separate practice may miss.')
],'Plan a club product: name the audience, purpose, format, two roles, one source-checking step and one revision checkpoint. Explain why the format fits.','Look for a coherent, feasible plan and a link between format and audience. Assess the written plan, not an unseen final performance.'),
lesson('feedback-reflection','projects','Useful feedback, real improvement','Connect a specific observation to a practical next step.',[10,16],[
 section('Observe before judging','The peer feedback sheet considers content, organisation, delivery, collaboration and creativity, using four points for each category. Give feedback only on what you can observe. A polished slide alone cannot prove equal collaboration. Ask for evidence when a process is not visible.','Observation: “Three speakers explained different parts and handed over clearly.” Avoid: “Everyone definitely did exactly equal work.”','قيّم ما شاهدته. جودة الشرائح لا تثبت وحدها عدالة توزيع العمل. فرّق بين الملاحظة والافتراض.'),
 section('Two stars and a useful wish','A helpful strength names what worked and why. A useful improvement is specific, respectful and possible to act on. Separate the work from the person. Explain how the change would help the audience.','“The example makes the claim concrete. Add a brief conclusion to connect it back to your main point.”','اذكر القوة وسبب فائدتها، ثم تعديلًا واضحًا قابلًا للتنفيذ. انتقد العمل باحترام دون تجريح الشخص.'),
 section('Reflect with evidence','Reflection goes beyond saying a task was easy or hard. Identify a challenge, the action you took, the outcome and your next step. Use feedback to select a priority. Different source rubrics measure different things: the peer sheet has five categories × four points, while the project rubric has four × five. Both total 20, but their criteria are not interchangeable.','“Our first rehearsal ran two minutes long. We cut a repeated example. Next time we will time the outline before making slides.”','التأمل: تحدٍّ ثم إجراء ثم نتيجة ثم خطوة لاحقة. المقاييس التي مجموعها واحد قد تختلف في المعايير.')
],[
 mc('Which claim can be supported by watching a presentation alone?','The speakers handed over clearly between sections.',['Every member spent exactly equal time researching.','Nobody disagreed during preparation.','All sources were checked unless shown.'],'Clear handovers are observable; hidden preparation processes need more evidence.'),
 mc('Which comment focuses respectfully on improving the work?','Add a conclusion that connects the example to your main point.',['You are a bad presenter.','Your team is hopeless.','Nothing can help.'],'The comment gives a concrete revision instead of judging the person.'),
 mc('What does meaningful reflection connect?','A challenge, an action, an outcome and a next step',['Only a grade and a complaint','Only the slide colours','A copied paragraph unrelated to the work'],'These elements explain learning and how it can shape future action.'),
 mc('The peer sheet has five categories worth four points each. What is its maximum total?','20',['9','16','25'],'Five multiplied by four equals 20.'),
 mc('Do two rubrics with a maximum of 20 necessarily measure the same criteria?','No; inspect the categories and descriptors.',['Yes; the total defines every criterion.','Yes; category names never matter.','No rubric can have five categories.'],'Equal totals do not make the criteria or expectations interchangeable.'),
 mc('Which next step follows most directly from a presentation running too long?','Time the outline and remove repeated points before rehearsing again.',['Add several unrelated examples.','Stop checking the time.','Make every slide more crowded.'],'Timing and removing repetition address the observed problem directly.')
],'Write feedback on a real or imagined presentation: two specific strengths and one improvement. Then write a reflection using challenge → action → outcome → next step. If imagined, label it as a scenario.','Assess specificity, respectful wording and the logic of the reflection. Do not treat imagined outcomes as observed classroom evidence.')
];
// Rotate choices deterministically so correct answers do not always occupy the first slot.
for(const [li,l] of schoolLessons.entries())for(const [qi,q] of l.questions.entries()){
 q.id=`${l.id}-q${qi+1}`;q.lesson=l.id;q.phase=qi<2?'practice':'quiz';
 if(q.type==='mc'){const offset=(li+qi)%q.options.length;q.options=[...q.options.slice(offset),...q.options.slice(0,offset)];q.answer=(q.options.length-offset)%q.options.length;}
}
export const schoolLessonById=Object.assign(Object.create(null),Object.fromEntries(schoolLessons.map(l=>[l.id,l])));
export const schoolQuestions=schoolLessons.flatMap(l=>l.questions);
export const schoolQuestionById=Object.assign(Object.create(null),Object.fromEntries(schoolQuestions.map(q=>[q.id,q])));
export const teacherRubric=[
 {id:'response',title:'Response & understanding',description:'Addresses the task accurately and presents a clear claim or purpose.'},
 {id:'evidence',title:'Evidence & examples',description:'Uses relevant textual details, examples or contextual support.'},
 {id:'reasoning',title:'Reasoning & organisation',description:'Explains connections and arranges ideas coherently.'},
 {id:'language',title:'Language & clarity',description:'Uses understandable, appropriate English; errors do not obscure the message.'}
];
export const officialIeltsURL='https://ielts.org/take-a-test/test-types/ielts-academic-test';
